  Modify the code that calls the query to wrap it in a transaction:

  BEGIN;
  SET LOCAL enable_hashjoin = off;
  SET LOCAL enable_mergejoin = off;

  -- Run the 3000-line query here
  SELECT ... ;

  COMMIT;
  SET LOCAL ensures that the "No Hash Join" rule only lasts for that one specific transaction. The moment the transaction ends, the setting is wiped, and there is zero
  risk to any other query in the system.



2. Change role
The role level change is bad

  A. The User-Level Fix (Safest)

  If you have a specific database user (role) that runs these reports, you can apply the "No Hash Join" rule only to that user. This prevents the risk from affecting other
  parts of your system.

  -- This only affects the 'report_user', not the whole DB
  ALTER ROLE report_user SET enable_hashjoin = off;
  ALTER ROLE report_user SET enable_mergejoin = off;
  Now, whenever report_user logs in, they are automatically in "Nested Loop mode," but the rest of the application continues to use Hash Joins.
